TAO = [
    (1, 'The Tao that can be trodden is not the enduring and unchanging Tao. The name that can be named is not the enduring and unchanging name.'),
    (1, 'Conceived of as having no name, it is the Originator of heaven and earth; conceived of as having a name, it is the Mother of all things.'),
    (1, 'Under these two aspects, it is really the same; but as development takes place, it receives the different names.'),
    (1, 'Where the Mystery is the deepest is the gate of all that is subtle and wonderful.'),
    (2, 'The work is accomplished, and there is no resting in it as an achievement.'),
    (2, "The work is done, but how no one can see; 'Tis this that makes the power not cease to be."),
    (3, 'When there is this abstinence from action, good order is universal.'),
    (4, 'The Tao is like the emptiness of a vessel; and in our employment of it we must be on our guard against all fulness.'),
    (4, 'I do not know whose son it is. It might appear to have been before God.'),
    (5, 'Heaven and earth do not act from the impulse of any wish to be benevolent; they deal with all things as the dogs of grass are dealt with.'),
    (5, 'The sages do not act from any wish to be benevolent; they deal with the people as the dogs of grass are dealt with.'),
    (5, 'May not the space between heaven and earth be compared to a bellows?'),
    (7, 'Heaven is long-enduring and earth continues long.'),
    (7, 'The reason why heaven and earth are able to endure and continue thus long is because they do not live of, or for, themselves.'),
    (7, 'Is it not because he has no personal and private ends, that therefore such ends are realised?'),
    (8, 'The highest excellence is like that of water.'),
    (8, 'And when one with the highest excellence does not wrangle about his low position, no one finds fault with him.'),
    (9, 'If you keep feeling a point that has been sharpened, the point cannot long preserve its sharpness.'),
    (9, 'When gold and jade fill the hall, their possessor cannot keep them safe.'),
    (9, 'When wealth and honours lead to arrogancy, this brings its evil on itself.'),
    (9, "When the work is done, and one's name is becoming distinguished, to withdraw into obscurity is the way of Heaven."),
    (10, 'When the intelligent and animal souls are held together in one embrace, they can be kept from separating.'),
    (10, 'When one gives undivided attention to the vital breath, and brings it to the utmost degree of pliancy, he can become as a tender babe.'),
    (10, 'When he has cleansed away the most mysterious sights of his imagination, he can become without a flaw.'),
    (10, 'In loving the people and ruling the state, cannot he proceed without any purpose of action?'),
    (10, 'In the opening and shutting of his gates of heaven, cannot he do so as a female bird?'),
    (10, 'While his intelligence reaches in every direction, cannot he appear to be without knowledge?'),
    (10, 'The thirty spokes unite in the one nave; but it is on the empty space for the axle, that the use of the wheel depends.'),
    (10, 'Clay is fashioned into vessels; but it is on their empty hollowness, that their use depends.'),
    (10, 'The door and windows are cut out from the walls to form an apartment; but it is on the empty space within, that its use depends.'),
    (10, 'He puts from him the latter, and prefers to seek the former.'),
    (13, 'Favour and disgrace would seem equally to be feared; honour and great calamity, to be regarded as personal conditions of the same kind.'),
    (13, 'What is meant by speaking thus of favour and disgrace?'),
    (13, 'Disgrace is being in a low position after the enjoyment of favour.'),
    (13, 'And what is meant by saying that honour and great calamity are to be similarly regarded as personal conditions?'),
    (13, 'What makes me liable to great calamity is my having the body which I call myself; if I had not the body, what great calamity could come to me?'),
    (14, 'Its upper part is not bright, and its lower part is not obscure.'),
    (14, 'Ceaseless in its action, it yet cannot be named, and then it again returns and becomes nothing.'),
    (14, 'We meet it and do not see its Front; we follow it, and do not see its Back.'),
    (15, "As they were thus beyond men's knowledge, I will make an effort to describe of what sort they appeared to be."),
    (15, 'Let it be still, and it will gradually become clear.'),
    (15, 'Let movement go on, and the condition of rest will gradually arise.'),
    (15, 'They who preserve this method of the Tao do not wish to be full of themselves.'),
    (16, 'The state of vacancy should be brought to the utmost degree, and that of stillness guarded with unwearying vigour.'),
    (16, 'All things alike go through their processes of activity, and then we see them return to their original state.'),
    (16, 'When things in the vegetable world have displayed their luxuriant growth, we see each of them return to its root.'),
    (16, 'The report of that fulfilment is the regular, unchanging rule.'),
    (16, 'To know that unchanging rule is to be intelligent; not to know it leads to wild movements and evil issues.'),
    (16, 'From this community of feeling comes a kingliness of character; and he who is king-like goes on to be heaven-like.'),
    (16, 'In that likeness to heaven he possesses the Tao.'),
    (16, 'Possessed of the Tao, he endures long; and to the end of his bodily life, is exempt from all danger of decay.'),
    (17, 'In the highest antiquity, the people did not know that there were their rulers.'),
    (17, 'In the next age they loved them and praised them.'),
    (17, 'In the next they feared them; in the next they despised them.'),
    (18, 'When the Great Tao Way or Method ceased to be observed, benevolence and righteousness came into vogue.'),
    (18, 'Then appeared wisdom and shrewdness, and there ensued great hypocrisy.'),
    (19, 'If we could renounce our sageness and discard our wisdom, it would be better for the people a hundredfold.'),
    (19, 'If we could renounce our benevolence and discard our righteousness, the people would again become filial and kindly.'),
    (19, 'If we could renounce our artful contrivances and discard our scheming for gain, there would be no thieves nor robbers.'),
    (19, 'What all men fear is indeed to be feared; but how wide and without end is the range of questions asking to be discussed!'),
    (19, 'The multitude of men look satisfied and pleased; as if enjoying a full banquet, as if mounted on a tower in spring.'),
    (19, 'I alone seem listless and still, my desires having as yet given no indication of their presence.'),
    (19, 'I am like an infant which has not yet smiled.'),
    (19, 'I look dejected and forlorn, as if I had no home to go to.'),
    (19, 'The multitude of men all have enough and to spare.'),
    (19, 'Ordinary men look bright and intelligent, while I alone seem to be benighted.'),
    (19, 'They look full of discrimination, while I alone am dull and confused.'),
    (19, 'I seem to be carried about as on the sea, drifting as if I had nowhere to rest.'),
    (19, 'All men have their spheres of action, while I alone seem dull and incapable, like a rude borderer.'),
    (22, 'The partial becomes complete; the crooked, straight; the empty, full; the worn out, new.'),
    (22, 'He whose desires are few gets them; he whose desires are many goes astray.'),
    (23, 'Abstaining from speech marks him who is obeying the spontaneity of his nature.'),
    (23, 'A violent wind does not last for a whole morning; a sudden rain does not last for the whole day.'),
    (23, 'To whom is it that these two things are owing?'),
    (23, 'If Heaven and Earth cannot make such spasmodic actings last long, how much less can man!'),
    (23, 'But when there is not faith sufficient on his part, a want of faith in him ensues on the part of the others.'),
    (23, 'He who stands on his tiptoes does not stand firm; he who stretches his legs does not walk easily.'),
    (25, 'There was something undefined and complete, coming into existence before Heaven and Earth.'),
    (25, 'It may be regarded as the Mother of all things.'),
    (25, 'I do not know its name, and I give it the designation of the Tao the Way or Course. Making an effort further to give it a name I call it The Great.'),
    (25, 'Earth is great; and the sage king is also great.'),
    (25, 'In the universe there are four that are great, and the sage king is one of them.'),
    (25, 'Man takes his law from the Earth; the Earth takes its law from Heaven; Heaven takes its law from the Tao. The law of the Tao is its being what it is.'),
    (26, 'Gravity is the root of lightness; stillness, the ruler of movement.'),
    (26, 'Although he may have brilliant prospects to look at, he quietly remains in his proper place, indifferent to them.'),
    (26, 'If he do act lightly, he has lost his root of gravity; if he proceed to active movement, he will lose his throne.'),
    (27, 'If the one did not honour his master, and the other did not rejoice in his helper, an observer, though intelligent, might greatly err about them.'),
    (27, 'The unwrought material, when divided and distributed, forms vessels.'),
    (27, 'The sage, when employed, becomes the Head of all the Officers of government; and in his greatest regulations he employs no violent measures.'),
    (29, 'If any one should wish to get the kingdom for himself, and to effect this by what he does, I see that he will not succeed.'),
    (29, 'The kingdom is a spirit-like thing, and cannot be got by active doing.'),
    (29, 'He who would so win it destroys it; he who would hold it in his grasp loses it.'),
    (30, 'He who would assist a lord of men in harmony with the Tao will not assert his mastery in the kingdom by force of arms.'),
    (30, 'Wherever a host is stationed, briars and thorns spring up. In the sequence of great armies there are sure to be bad years.'),
    (30, 'A skilful commander strikes a decisive blow, and stops.'),
    (30, 'He does not dare by continuing his operations to assert and complete his mastery.'),
    (30, 'He will strike the blow, but will be on his guard against being vain or boastful or arrogant in consequence of it.'),
    (30, 'He strikes it as a matter of necessity; he strikes it, but not from a wish for mastery.'),
    (30, 'When things have attained their strong maturity they become old.'),
    (31, 'Now arms, however beautiful, are instruments of evil omen, hateful, it may be said, to all creatures.'),
    (31, 'The superior man ordinarily considers the left hand the most honourable place, but in time of war the right hand.'),
    (31, 'Those sharp weapons are instruments of evil omen, and not the instruments of the superior man;--he uses them only on the compulsion of necessity.'),
    (31, 'Calm and repose are what he prizes; victory by force of arms is to him undesirable.'),
    (31, 'On occasions of festivity to be on the left hand is the prized position; on occasions of mourning, the right hand.'),
    (32, 'The Tao, considered as unchanging, has no name.'),
    (32, 'Though in its primordial simplicity it may be small, the whole world dares not deal with one embodying it as a minister.'),
    (32, 'If a feudal prince or the king could guard and hold it, all would spontaneously submit themselves to him.'),
    (32, 'As soon as it proceeds to action, it has a name.'),
    (32, 'When it once has that name, men can know to rest in it.'),
    (32, 'When they know to rest in it, they can be free from all risk of failure and error.'),
    (32, 'The relation of the Tao to all the world is like that of the great rivers and seas to the streams from the valleys.'),
    (33, 'He who knows other men is discerning; he who knows himself is intelligent.'),
    (33, 'He who overcomes others is strong; he who overcomes himself is mighty.'),
    (33, 'He who is satisfied with his lot is rich; he who goes on acting with energy has a firm will.'),
    (33, 'He who does not fail in the requirements of his position, continues long; he who dies and yet does not perish, has longevity.'),
    (34, 'All-pervading is the Great Tao! It may be found on the left hand and on the right.'),
    (34, 'All things depend on it for their production, which it gives to them, not one refusing obedience to it.'),
    (34, 'When its work is accomplished, it does not claim the name of having done it.'),
    (34, 'It clothes all things as with a garment, and makes no assumption of being their lord;--it may be named in the smallest things.'),
    (35, 'To him who holds in his hands the Great Image of the invisible Tao, the whole world repairs.'),
    (35, 'Men resort to him, and receive no hurt, but find rest, peace, and the feeling of ease.'),
    (35, 'Music and dainties will make the passing guest stop for a time.'),
    (36, 'The soft overcomes the hard; and the weak the strong.'),
    (36, 'Fishes should not be taken from the deep; instruments for the profit of a state should not be shown to the people.'),
    (37, 'The Tao in its regular course does nothing for the sake of doing it, and so there is nothing which it does not do.'),
    (37, 'If princes and kings were able to maintain it, all things would of themselves be transformed by them.'),
    (37, 'If this transformation became to me an object of desire, I would express the desire by the nameless simplicity.'),
    (37, 'Simplicity without a name Is free from all external aim. With no desire, at rest and still, All things go right as of their will.'),
    (38, 'Those who possessed in highest degree the attributes of the Tao did not seek to show them, and therefore they possessed them in fullest measure.'),
    (38, 'Those who possessed in a lower degree those attributes sought how not to lose them, and therefore they did not possess them in fullest measure.'),
    (38, 'Those who possessed in the highest degree those attributes did nothing with a purpose, and had no need to do anything.'),
    (38, 'Those who possessed them in a lower degree were always doing, and had need to be so doing.'),
    (38, 'Those who possessed the highest benevolence were always seeking to carry it out, and had no need to be doing so.'),
    (38, 'Those who possessed the highest righteousness were always seeking to carry it out, and had need to be so doing.'),
    (39, 'The things which from of old have got the One the Tao are--'),
    (39, 'They do not wish to show themselves elegant-looking as jade, but prefer to be coarse-looking as an ordinary stone.'),
    (39, "The movement of the Tao By contraries proceeds; And weakness marks the course Of Tao's mighty deeds."),
    (39, 'All things under heaven sprang from It as existing and named; that existence sprang from It as non-existent and not named.'),
    (41, 'Scholars of the highest class, when they hear about the Tao, earnestly carry it into practice.'),
    (41, 'Scholars of the middle class, when they have heard about it, seem now to keep it and now to lose it.'),
    (41, 'Scholars of the lowest class, when they have heard about it, laugh greatly at it.'),
    (41, 'If it were not thus laughed at, it would not be fit to be the Tao.'),
    (41, 'And he has most whose lot the least supplies.'),
    (41, 'The Tao is hidden, and has no name; but it is the Tao which is skilful at imparting to all things what they need and making them complete.'),
    (42, 'What other men thus teach, I also teach. The violent and strong do not die their natural death. I will make this the basis of my teaching.'),
    (43, 'The softest thing in the world dashes against and overcomes the hardest; that which has no substantial existence enters where there is no crevice.'),
    (43, 'I know hereby what advantage belongs to doing nothing with a purpose.'),
    (43, 'There are few in the world who attain to the teaching without words, and the advantage arising from non-action.'),
    (43, 'Who is content Needs fear no shame. Who knows to stop Incurs no blame. From danger free Long live shall he.'),
    (43, 'Constant action overcomes cold; being still overcomes heat. Purity and stillness give the correct law to all under heaven.'),
    (46, 'When the Tao prevails in the world, they send back their swift horses to draw the dung-carts.'),
    (46, 'When the Tao is disregarded in the world, the war-horses breed in the border lands.'),
    (47, 'Without going outside his door, one understands all that takes place under the sky; without looking out from his window, one sees the Tao of Heaven.'),
    (47, 'The farther that one goes out from himself, the less he knows.'),
    (48, 'He diminishes it and again diminishes it, till he arrives at doing nothing on purpose.'),
    (48, 'Having arrived at this point of non-action, there is nothing which he does not do.'),
    (48, 'He who gets as his own all under heaven does so by giving himself no trouble with that end.'),
    (48, 'If one take trouble with that end, he is not equal to getting as his own all under heaven.'),
    (49, 'The sage has no invariable mind of his own; he makes the mind of the people his mind.'),
    (49, 'To those who are good to me, I am good; and to those who are not good to me, I am also good;--and thus all get to be good.'),
    (49, 'To those who are sincere with me, I am sincere; and to those who are not sincere with me, I am also sincere;--and thus all get to be sincere.'),
    (49, 'The sage has in the world an appearance of indecision, and keeps his mind in a state of indifference to all.'),
    (49, 'The people all keep their eyes and ears directed to him, and he deals with them all as his children.'),
    (50, 'Men come forth and live; they enter again and die.'),
    (50, 'Of every ten three are ministers of life to themselves; and three are ministers of death.'),
    (50, 'There are also three in every ten whose aim is to live, but whose movements tend to the land or place of death.'),
    (50, 'Because of their excessive endeavours to perpetuate life.'),
    (51, 'All things are produced by the Tao, and nourished by its outflowing operation.'),
    (51, 'They receive their forms according to the nature of each, and are completed according to the circumstances of their condition.'),
    (52, 'The Tao which originated all under the sky is to be considered as the mother of them all.'),
    (52, 'When the mother is found, we know what her children should be.'),
    (52, 'Let him keep his mouth closed, and shut up the portals of his nostrils, and all his life he will be exempt from laborious exertion.'),
    (52, 'Let him keep his mouth open, and spend his breath in the promotion of his affairs, and all his life there will be no safety for him.'),
    (52, 'The perception of what is small is the secret of clear-sightedness; the guarding of what is soft and tender is the secret of strength.'),
    (52, "Who uses well his light, Reverting to its source so bright, Will from his body ward all blight, And hides the unchanging from men's sight."),
    (53, 'The great Tao or way is very level and easy; but people love the by-ways.'),
    (53, 'Their court-yards and buildings shall be well kept, but their fields shall be ill-cultivated, and their granaries very empty.'),
    (55, 'He who has in himself abundantly the attributes of the Tao is like an infant.'),
    (55, 'Poisonous insects will not sting him; fierce beasts will not seize him; birds of prey will not strike him.'),
    (55, "The infant's bones are weak and its sinews soft, but yet its grasp is firm."),
    (55, 'It knows not yet the union of male and female, and yet its virile member may be excited;--showing the perfection of its physical essence.'),
    (55, 'All day long it will cry without its throat becoming hoarse;--showing the harmony in its constitution.'),
    (55, 'When things have become strong, they then become old, which may be said to be contrary to the Tao. Whatever is contrary to the Tao soon ends.'),
    (56, 'He who knows the Tao does not care to speak about it; he who is ever ready to speak about it does not know it.'),
    (56, 'He who knows it will keep his mouth shut and close the portals of his nostrils.'),
    (57, 'I will be fond of keeping still, and the people will of themselves become correct.'),
    (57, 'Misery!--happiness is to be found by its side! Happiness!--misery lurks beneath it! Who knows what either will come to in the end?'),
    (57, 'The method of correction shall by a turn become distortion, and the good in it shall by a turn become evil.'),
    (57, 'The delusion of the people on this point has indeed subsisted for a long time.'),
    (57, 'He is straightforward, but allows himself no license; he is bright, but does not dazzle.'),
    (59, 'For regulating the human in our constitution and rendering the proper service to the heavenly, there is nothing like moderation.'),
    (59, 'With that repeated accumulation of those attributes, there comes the subjugation of every obstacle to such return.'),
    (59, 'Of this subjugation we know not what shall be the limit; and when one knows not what the limit shall be, he may be the ruler of a state.'),
    (59, 'He who possesses the mother of the state may continue long.'),
    (60, 'Governing a great state is like cooking small fish.'),
    (60, 'Let the kingdom be governed according to the Tao, and the manes of the departed will not manifest their spiritual energy.'),
    (60, 'When these two do not injuriously affect each other, their good influences converge in the virtue of the Tao.'),
    (61, 'What makes a great state is its being like a low-lying, down-flowing stream;--it becomes the centre to which tend all the small states under heaven.'),
    (61, 'To illustrate from the case of all females:--the female always overcomes the male by her stillness. Stillness may be considered a sort of abasement.'),
    (61, 'In the one case the abasement leads to gaining adherents, in the other case to procuring favour.'),
    (61, 'The great state only wishes to unite men together and nourish them; a small state only wishes to be received by, and to serve, the other.'),
    (61, 'Each gets what it desires, but the great state must learn to abase itself.'),
    (61, 'Tao has of all things the most honoured place. No treasures give good men so rich a grace; Bad men it guards, and doth their ill efface.'),
    (61, 'Its admirable words can purchase honour; its admirable deeds can raise their performer above others.'),
    (61, 'Even men who are not good are not abandoned by it.'),
    (61, 'Was it not because it could be got by seeking for it, and the guilty could escape from the stain of their guilt by it?'),
    (63, 'The master of it anticipates things that are difficult while they are easy, and does things that would become great while they are small.'),
    (63, 'He who lightly promises is sure to keep but little faith; he who is continually thinking things easy is sure to find them difficult.'),
    (64, 'Action should be taken before a thing has made its appearance; order should be secured before disorder has begun.'),
    (64, 'He who acts with an ulterior purpose does harm; he who takes hold of a thing in the same way loses his hold.'),
    (64, 'The sage does not act so, and therefore does no harm; he does not lay hold so, and therefore does not lose his hold.'),
    (64, 'But people in their conduct of affairs are constantly ruining them when they are on the eve of success.'),
    (64, 'If they were careful at the end, as they should be at the beginning, they would not so ruin them.'),
    (65, 'The ancients who showed their skill in practising the Tao did so, not to enlighten the people, but rather to make them simple and ignorant.'),
    (65, 'The difficulty in governing the people arises from their having much knowledge.'),
    (65, 'He who tries to govern a state by his wisdom is a scourge to it; while he who does not try to do so is a blessing.'),
    (65, 'He who knows these two things finds in them also his model and rule.'),
    (65, 'Ability to know this model and rule constitutes what we call the mysterious excellence of a governor.'),
    (67, 'All the world says that, while my Tao is great, it yet appears to be inferior to other systems of teaching.'),
    (67, 'Now it is just its greatness that makes it seem to be inferior.'),
    (67, 'If it were like any other system, for long would its smallness have been known!'),
    (67, 'But I have three precious things which I prize and hold fast.'),
    (67, 'The first is gentleness; the second is economy; and the third is shrinking from taking precedence of others.'),
    (67, 'Gentleness is sure to be victorious even in battle, and firmly to maintain its ground.'),
    (67, 'Heaven will save its possessor, by his very gentleness protecting him.'),
    (69, 'I prefer to be the guest to act on the defensive.'),
    (69, 'There is no calamity greater than lightly engaging in war.'),
    (69, 'To do that is near losing the gentleness which is so precious.'),
    (70, 'My words are very easy to know, and very easy to practise; but there is no one in the world who is able to know and able to practise them.'),
    (70, 'There is an originating and all-comprehending principle in my words, and an authoritative law for the things which I enforce.'),
    (70, 'They who know me are few, and I am on that account the more to be prized.'),
    (71, 'To know and yet think we do not know is the highest attainment; not to know and yet think we do know is a disease.'),
    (71, 'He knows the pain that would be inseparable from it, and therefore he does not have it.'),
    (72, 'When the people do not fear what they ought to fear, that which is their great dread will come on them.'),
    (72, 'Let them not thoughtlessly indulge themselves in their ordinary life; let them not act as if weary of what that life depends on.'),
    (72, 'And thus he puts the latter alternative away and makes choice of the former.'),
    (73, 'Of these two cases the one appears to be advantageous, and the other to be injurious.'),
    (73, "When Heaven's anger smites a man, Who the cause shall truly scan?"),
    (73, 'On this account the sage feels a difficulty as to what to do in the former case.'),
    (73, 'Its demonstrations are quiet, and yet its plans are skilful and effective.'),
    (73, 'The meshes of the net of Heaven are large; far apart, but letting nothing escape.'),
    (74, 'The people do not fear death; to what purpose is it to try to frighten them with death?'),
    (74, 'If the people were always in awe of death, and I could always seize those who do wrong, and put them to death, who would dare to do wrong?'),
    (74, 'There is always One who presides over the infliction of death.'),
    (74, 'He who would inflict death in the room of him who so presides over it may be described as hewing wood instead of a great carpenter.'),
    (74, 'Seldom is it that he who undertakes the hewing, instead of the great carpenter, does not cut his own hands!'),
    (75, 'The people suffer from famine because of the multitude of taxes consumed by their superiors. It is through this that they suffer famine.'),
    (75, 'The people are difficult to govern because of the excessive agency of their superiors in governing them.'),
    (75, 'The people make light of dying because of the greatness of their labours in seeking for the means of living.'),
    (76, 'Man at his birth is supple and weak; at his death, firm and strong.'),
    (76, 'Trees and plants, in their early growth, are soft and brittle; at their death, dry and withered.'),
    (77, 'May not the Way or Tao of Heaven be compared to the method of bending a bow?'),
    (77, 'The part of the bow which was high is brought low, and what was low is raised up.'),
    (77, 'So Heaven diminishes where there is superabundance, and supplements where there is deficiency.'),
    (77, 'He takes away from those who have not enough to add to his own superabundance.'),
    (77, 'Who can take his own superabundance and therewith serve all under heaven? Only he who is in possession of the Tao!'),
    (78, 'Every one in the world knows that the soft overcomes the hard, and the weak the strong, but no one is able to carry it out in practice.'),
    (78, 'Words that are strictly true seem to be paradoxical.'),
    (79, 'In the Way of Heaven, there is no partiality of love; it is always on the side of the good man.'),
    (80, 'I would make the people, while looking on death as a grievous thing, yet not remove elsewhere to avoid it.'),
    (80, 'I would make the people return to the use of knotted cords instead of the written characters.'),
    (81, 'Sincere words are not fine; fine words are not sincere.'),
    (81, 'Those who are skilled in the Tao do not dispute about it; the disputatious are not skilled in it.'),
    (81, 'Those who know the Tao are not extensively learned; the extensively learned do not know it.'),
    (81, 'The more that he expends for others, the more does he possess of his own; the more that he gives to others, the more does he have himself.'),
    (81, 'With all the sharpness of the Way of Heaven, it injures not; with all the doing in the way of the sage he does not strive.'),
]
